<p>Global military spending rose 2.9% in real terms to $2.887 trillion in 2025, marking the 11th consecutive annual increase and pushing the military burden to 2.5% of global GDP, its highest level since 2009, according to the Stockholm International Peace Research Institute&#x27;s annual report published Monday. The United States remained the world&#x27;s biggest military spender, but its outlays fell 7.5% after President Donald Trump halted new financial military aid to Ukraine, while Europe and Asia-Oceania posted much stronger gains. Regional trends drove much of the increase. Spending in Europe rose 14%, and Asia and Oceania rose 8.1%, with SIPRI and cited analysts linking the Asia-Pacific jump to growing uncertainty among US allies about Washington&#x27;s long-term security commitments. The three biggest spenders — the United States, China and Russia — accounted for a combined $1.48 trillion, just over half of global expenditure, underscoring how concentrated defense budgets remain even as many countries expand their own rearmament efforts.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Kw2H5XO9HunxRyDKIR44zurWxsWy__a0jVs4kQ9elHry4Uyk676fPJzoo-zoqPV9_PFRQmZI5HDTe497C2heZVz9O8Ax_irIYWRjQ4l-ln5N' alt='Tanks and an armoured vehicle drive in Lebanon, as seen from the Israeli side of the Israel-Lebanon border, in northern Israel, April 25, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Middle East: Military spending in the Middle East was broadly flat at an estimated $218 billion in 2025, up just 0.1% from 2024 despite continuing conflicts and regional rivalries.</li><li>Israel shift: Israel&#x27;s military expenditure fell 4.9% to $48.3 billion after the Gaza conflict eased in intensity during 2025 following a ceasefire with Hamas, though spending remained 97% above its 2022 level.</li><li>Turkey rise: Turkey increased military spending 7.2% to $30.0 billion, with SIPRI linking the rise in part to its ongoing military operations in Iraq, Somalia and Syria.</li><li>Iran pressures: Iran&#x27;s military spending fell 5.6% in real terms to $7.4 billion for a second straight year. SIPRI researcher Zubaida Karim said that reflected economic difficulties and 42% annual inflation, even though nominal spending rose.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>SIPRI analysts: SIPRI&#x27;s report and cited researchers described the 2025 increase as part of a broader rearmament trend driven by insecurity, with DW noting that the institute expects the pattern to continue and warns it carries additional risks. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/sipri-record-arms-spending-again/a-76901397'>DW</a>)</li><li>Asia-Pacific US allies: Countries in the Asia-Pacific increased spending at the fastest pace since 2009 amid what the report described as growing uncertainty over whether Washington would honor its security commitments. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/china/military/article/3351456/trump-factor-drives-asia-pacific-military-spending-fastest-pace-2009'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/china/military/article/3351456/trump-factor-drives-asia-pacific-military-spending-fastest-pace-2009'>Trump factor drives up Asia-Pacific military spending at fastest pace since 2009</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/global-military-spending-rises-29-despite-us-decline-over-ukraine-freeze'>Global military spending rises 2.9% despite US decline over Ukraine freeze</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/sipri-record-arms-spending-again/a-76901397'>SIPRI: Record arms spending, again</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1995443/global-military-spending-continues-to-rise-as-european-and-asian-expenditures-surge'>Global military spending continues to rise as European and Asian expenditures surge</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/global-military-spending-rises-2-9-despite-us-decline-over-ukraine-freeze'>Global military spending surges on insecurity: Report</a> - straitstimes.com</li></ul>